      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Meta introduces Spaces, a new way to collaborate with your Muse agent",
      "d": "Meta's Muse agents continue to get new features and integrations. Spaces promises to be a new way to interact with the AI helpers.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/06/meta-introduces-spaces-a-new-way-to-collaborate-with-your-muse-agent/",
      "m": "11. 06. PM 06:44",
      "ts": 1793990660000,
      "fullText": "Meta recently launched its Muse agents — a new generation of cuddly agentic helpers. Now, the company has launched Spaces, a new way to communicate and collaborate with them. As far as I can tell, Spaces are basically breakout conversations that revolve around a particular topic — although Meta refers to them as &#8220;custom experiences.&#8221; They live in the sidebar of the interface where you interact with your Muse agent. Clicking on the Space brings up the conversation, where Muse works toward a particular goal on your behalf. For instance, if you wanted to plan a vacation, you could open a new Space focused on that particular project, and whenever you want to work on it with Muse, you would click to open that particular tab. Or, if you&#8217;re focused on collecting recipes for an upcoming family event, you&#8217;d open a new Space for that particular project. Other examples Meta gives are a holiday gift tracker, a wedding planner, or a space for DIY projects. &#8220;Describe an idea in chat, and Muse builds a space for you, whether that’s a tracker, a planner or a project board,&#8221; Meta says. Image Credits: Meta Spaces can also be easily integrated with third-party apps and information, Meta says. &#8220;Connect the apps you use, and your spaces keep themselves up to date in the background, so new concert dates, price changes, or savings progress show up without you having to ask,&#8221; the company says. On Friday, Mark Zuckerberg, Meta&#8217;s founder and CEO, posted about the new feature on X: &#8220;Playing around with spaces in Muse lately &#8212; tell Muse what you need and it builds it. I&#8217;ve made a BBQ game, an event tracker, and a home control dashboard. Excited to see what you build.&#8221; Muse agents — and the cuddly avatars that enclose them — have been greeted with a relatively warm reception over the past few months. During its earnings call in October, Zuckerberg said that its agents had 10 million users in the U.S. and Canada — less"
    },
    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Tesla will stop making the Cybertruck in 2027",
      "d": "The decision to wind down the EV comes after just a few short years of underwhelming sales. Elon Musk said the company will use the factory space to make more Cybercabs.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/06/tesla-will-stop-making-the-cybertruck-in-2027/",
      "m": "11. 06. AM 11:50",
      "ts": 1793965810000,
      "fullText": "Tesla will stop making the Cybertruck next year, according to CEO Elon Musk, who said on X that the company will use the factory space to build more two-seater Cybercab robotaxis. The decision to wind down the futuristic, steel-clad EV comes after just a few short years of underwhelming sales. When Musk handed over the first few Cybertrucks in late 2023, he predicted Tesla would sell around 250,000 per year. It was supposed to be such a cornerstone of Tesla&#8217;s future that, before construction began, the company talked about its Texas factory as the &#8220;Cybertruck factory.&#8221; But Tesla has instead sold just tens of thousands &#8212; total &#8212; over the last three years. Tesla is killing off the Cybertruck at a moment when it is trying to become an AI- and robotics-focused company. A big part of that is its robotaxi ambitions. The company has spent the last two months putting Cybercabs on the streets in its hometown of Austin, Texas. With no major incidents to date, the company has said it plans to expand the Cybercabs operations to other cities, starting with Dallas later this month. (Tesla also operates robotaxi versions of its Model Y SUVs in Austin, Houston, and Dallas, as well as three cities in Florida.) This is the third product Tesla has discontinued in order to fuel those greater ambitions. Earlier this year, the company decided to end production of the Model S sedan and Model X SUV and instead use the factory space dedicated to those vehicles to make Optimus humanoid robots. First announced in 2019, the Cybertruck was a product that Musk had long obsessed over. &#8220;Probably my personal favorite for the next product is the pickup truck, and we are going to just do an amazing pickup truck,&#8221; he said on one of Tesla&#8217;s earnings calls that year. Musk promised the Cybertruck would be radical, inspired by the designs of cyberpunk sci-fi stories like &#8220;Blade Runner.&#8221; While that certainly bore out in the design, the company ove"
